<div class="heraldic-chain">
  <p class="intro"><strong>Visual reference.</strong> This page adapts the Claude Design export into a static Eleventy-friendly infographic. The evidence map is presented as a reference visual, not as a replacement for the underlying research files and source analysis.</p>
  <div class="heraldic-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable heraldic chain infographic">
    <section class="chain-board" aria-label="Heraldic chain of evidence across G14 through G29">
      <div class="chain-title">
        <div>
          <h2>A Heraldic Chain of Evidence for the Gurney Line</h2>
          <div class="chain-key"><span>Documented marker</span><span>Open or inferential marker</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>This infographic shows that the Gurney lineage is not supported only by written records. Across roughly fifteen generations, the same family shield and related heraldic displays appear again and again in seals, stained glass, churches, houses, monuments, and armorial pedigrees — creating a physical evidence chain that independently ties the Tudor Gurneys to the medieval Norfolk line.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chain-head">
        <div></div>
        <div class="evidence-head visitations"><div class="evidence-icon"></div><h2>Heralds’ Visitations</h2><p>Formal Tudor pedigree reviews record the later Gurney branch — the bridge into the London generations.</p></div>
        <div class="evidence-head west"><div class="evidence-icon"></div><h2>West Barsham Church Arms</h2><p>A public church display preserved this family’s marriage network in heraldic form.</p></div>
        <div class="evidence-head homes"><div class="evidence-icon"></div><h2>Shields in Homes</h2><p>Heraldic shields displayed inside family houses acted as a visible résumé of key marriages and inherited connections.</p></div>
        <div class="evidence-head walsingham"><div class="evidence-icon"></div><h2>Walsingham Abbey Glass</h2><p>A quartered shield in the abbey glass compresses several inherited family lines into one map of descent and inheritance.</p></div>
        <div class="evidence-head recurring"><div class="evidence-icon"></div><h2>Recurring Gurney Arms</h2><p>The same red engrailed cross recurs across centuries and source types, tying the Tudor family back to the medieval Norfolk line.</p></div>
      </div>
      {% for row in rows %}
      <div class="chain-row">
        <div class="ancestor-cell"><span class="gen">{{ row.gen }}</span><span class="person"><strong>{{ row.name }}</strong><em class="{{ row.wifeClass }}">{{ row.wife }}</em></span></div>
        <div class="evidence-cell visitations {{ row.visitations }}"><span></span></div>
        <div class="evidence-cell west {{ row.west }}"><span></span></div>
        <div class="evidence-cell homes {{ row.homes }}"><span></span></div>
        <div class="evidence-cell walsingham {{ row.walsingham }}"><span></span></div>
        <div class="evidence-cell recurring {{ row.recurring }}"><span></span></div>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </section>
  </div>
  <section class="chain-summary" aria-label="Plain text summary">
    <h2>Plain text summary</h2>
    <p>The visual tracks G14 through G29 and shows five categories of heraldic witness: formal Heralds’ Visitations, West Barsham church arms, shields displayed in homes, Walsingham Abbey stained-glass arms, and the recurring Gurney arms. The pattern presented is that multiple independent heraldic contexts cluster around the same Tudor and medieval Norfolk Gurney descent rather than relying on a single compiled pedigree.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Later Tudor bridge:</strong> Visitations and West Barsham evidence cluster around G14–G17.</li>
      <li><strong>West Barsham marriage network:</strong> Church and house displays mark Wauncy, Calthorpe, Lovell, Holdich, Blennerhasset, Jerningham, Heydon, and related marriage/inheritance links.</li>
      <li><strong>Medieval armorial continuity:</strong> Walsingham glass, early arms and seal evidence, and recurring Gurney arms extend the chain back through the medieval Norfolk line.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
